// @check
// @error unknown type 'nope' within '{foo:string}|{bar:nope}'
// @error cannot infer a return type for 'f'
// CONTRACT (must-error): an UNRESOLVED type name inside an inline-object member of a
// UNION is a clean "unknown type" diagnostic, not a compiler crash. The inline-object
// arm of the type resolver poisons the whole annotation when a field type does not
// resolve — the same rule the array arm applies to its element — so the -1 sentinel
// never reaches `objFieldTypes`, where the union-arm seed gate indexes the type arena
// by a field type and would trap on it (`out of bounds array access`).
//
// The message now names the OFFENDING COMPONENT and keeps the composite as context
// (`'nope' within '{foo:string}|{bar:nope}'`, was the whole composite alone): the
// resolvers bank the innermost failing spelling (`unkTyPart`), so the diagnostic stops
// degrading as the annotation nests.
//
// NB THE SECOND DIAGNOSTIC. This file has pinned BOTH errors for the UNION shape since it
// was written, which refutes a report that the union case "loses the second diagnostic
// entirely" — re-graded at 3 annotations × 7 function bodies, whether `cannot infer a
// return type` accompanies the message is decided by the BODY, identically for `any`,
// `{bar: any}` and `{foo: string} | {bar: any}`.
function f(v: {foo: string} | {bar: nope}) { return v.bar }
print(f({ bar: 1 }))
